The ICE method is a tabular method that helps compute the extent of a reaction, represented by the unknown variable $x$, for a given equilibrium. It requires the filling of a table, called the ICE table. Each column of this table represents a reactant or a product, whereas the rows represent the different stages of a reaction reaching equilibrium: initial, change, and equilibrium, with the first row displaying the chemical reaction whose equilibrium we are interested in studying. All quantities in the table are expressed in amount of matter units (for example, molar or atmosphere units). Remember that both molarity and gas pressure inform about the amount of matter and can be unified in a more general property called the \emph{activity}.

There are a few rules to follow when filling the  ICE table. First, the values of $x$, the extent of the reaction, should respect the reaction's stoichiometry and, for example, if the stoichiometric coefficient of a reactant is 2, the corresponding entry on the table for that reactant should be 2$x$. Second, the entry should include negative signs for reactants and positive signs for the products. This is because reactants decompose, disappear to produce products. Also, the state of matter does matter, and solids or liquids should not be included in the table. The last, perhaps more important, row is the equilibrium, E, row. It contains the result of adding the I and C rows. 

Below we have examples of ICE charts for the reaction between carbon monoxide and water to produce carbon dioxide, with initial concentrations of 0.1M and 0.2 M, respectively, for carbon monoxide and water.
%%%%%%ICE CHART
\newcolumntype{J}{>{\small\raggedright\arraybackslash}X}
\begin{tcolorbox}[tab1,tabularx={J|Y YY Y Y   Y  Y   }]
  & \ce{CO_{(g)}}     &$+$& \ce{H_2O_{(g)}}     & $\ce{<=>}$    & \ce{CO_2_{(g)}}     &$+$ & \ce{H2_{(g)}}      \\\hline\hline
Inicial   & 0.1M && 0.2M &    & 0M   &  &0M\\
Change & $-x$ && $-x$ &    &  $x$ & &$x$ \\
Equilibrium  & 0.1$-x$ && 0.2$-$$x$ &    &  $x$ & &$x$ \\ 
\end{tcolorbox}
%%%%%%ICE CHART
Another example for for the decomposition of nitrosyl chloride (\ce{2NOCl_{(g)}})--used primarily as a chemical intermediate, a reagent in organic synthesis, and historically for flour bleaching--at an initial concentration of 0.3M to produce nitrogen monoxide and chlorine gas
